Z||000014||pred1tor83||01-31-2001||06:46 PM||||hmmm . . . thought wet sanding was done after you painted it? ? ? anywayz. . . <P>could you hook me up with some information about it (wet sanding)???  <P>thanx<P><BR>||||reg||3
Z||000015||rogergarrison||01-31-2001||06:51 PM||||I just hope you plan on putting the body parts back together before the final painting after you get it all trimmed in. I see so many who paint them apart, scratch them up, and get missmatched color on panels. Thats not such a big problem with solid colors though. Definate no-no with pearls, candys and glamor colors. With these new paints, you can just add an extra coat on one panel and in the sun, itll stick out like a sore thumb. Show us your pics when youve got it done.||||reg||1
Z||000016||rogergarrison||01-31-2001||07:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pred1tor83:<BR><B>hmmm . . . thought wet sanding was done after you painted it? ? ? anywayz. . . <P>could you hook me up with some information about it (wet sanding)???  <P>thanx<P>You can wet sand before painting if you want but its not a necessity. I dont. I sand with open coat #400, then paint. It wont matter if its not smooth as glass, because your gonna wet sand it after anyway. After clear has dried at least a couple of days. I use #1000 or #1200 wet or dry with plenty of water and a touch of dish soap. dry parts frequently to check for remaining orange peel. Itll look dull with shiny spots. sand till its all dull. Then I use machine buffer, not a power polisher with 3M super duty rubbing compound, then go over it again with finishing compound. then polish and wax it. I use Turtle Wax (special non-swirl) for the finish wax. Make sure whoever buffs with the buffer knows how and what there doing, ive seen many paint jobs ruined buffing the edges. Hand rub all the little nooks and crannys before you wax it too.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||jch82||01-31-2001||10:54 PM||||Wet sanding should be done before you paint. 400 to 500 grit is sufficient unless you get spots where the clear coat has came off. Then you should start with about 220grit and work your way up to 400. Now the car is ready to be painted. After the painting is done, you have to let the clear coat dry a few days. When it is completely dry, you should wet sand again with about 1200 grit. This removes any minor imperfections in the clear coat. The hardest part is buffing it smooth. You have to have someone that is experienced with buffing cars or else you can really screw things up. When the car is buffed, go over it with a nice car wax for the finishing touches. If done right, it will look shiny as glass!<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Before you criticize someone, walk a mile in his shoes. That way, if he  gets angry, he'll be a mile way - and barefoot....||||reg||1
Z||000018||Chris McDonald||02-01-2001||01:22 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by MooCow:<BR>[B]Well this is what my car looked like when I got it from ED.<BR> <P>Why are you pulling the body panels off?  Is this for a complete color change or are you replacing them with something else?<P>I always start by washing the car with a heavy detergent soap. Then it gets a chemical bath of laquer/enamel cleaner (dupont). Then the sanding.  If it is already in decent shape I wet sand with 320 to 400 with a little soap to continue breaking out any wax or grease.  You only need to prime (Eurothane is best) the areas that break through the clear or top coat.  If changing color I typically will seal the car and top coat with a high quality base/clear system.  If you do your homework and plenty of prep the final wet sanding and buffing will be minimal.  Planning the solvent temp of the paint with the tempurature conditions when painting will give the best results to avoid orange peal.<P>If you have any silicone, grease, or wax in the original paint, dry sanding will actually spread it around to other areas.  You usually find out the hard way when the new paint job lifts or bubbles either right away or at a later date.  Unless you know the orginal surface to be virgin pure, I would not risk a dry sand prep.  Leave that to Maaco.||||reg||1
